nodecode-debug-desk is a rewrite of aydnOktay's aydnOktay/hermes-debug-desk:

  https://github.com/aydnOktay/hermes-debug-desk
  commit 57cbd4d23eba978dc9a53ab6387a84c336992b74
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 debug-desk contributors (LICENSE)

The rewrite is the Hermes plugin's Python, as a Nodecode cell. What it keeps:
the two tools with their names and their descriptions (debug_daily_report,
debug_last_failure); the digest's shape and its rules — git log since local
midnight, the branch, the conventional-commit type counts with "other" and
"wip" folded in as the plugin folded them, the distinct touched files to 40,
the subjects to 15, no model call; the observer that runs after a tool call
and keeps a failure — a nonzero exit or traceback-like output, the last eight
lines clipped to 240 characters, common tokens and keys redacted before
anything is written, the same signature inside 120 seconds counted as one
failure rather than a new one; the optional written summary, on the user's own
model, with the plugin's three-sentence instruction; /debug-desk today | last
[summarize]; and the state kept outside the install tree.

What is left out, and why: the desktop pane, the `desk` status chip and the
dashboard's /board and /summarize routes are Hermes UI, and a Nodecode cell
owns no pane — the same data answers as text instead. The plugin's range
(expiring a failure, the 15-minute "fresh" chip) survives only as the
"(fresh)" mark in the answer. Watching Hermes's `terminal` and `process` tools
is watching the shell tool calls this engine makes; there is no other
process to watch.

Nothing of the original's Python is bundled. The cell is Common Lisp, it runs
the `git` the user already has, and it answers to its own state file.
